# E0120: variant or method used as the other

The methods of a struct or an enum are called on a value, `e.eval()`. The variants of an enum are built through the enum, `Expr.Num(1)`. A method named through its type, or a variant called on a value, is an error.

## Why

Sisu has one way to build a variant and one way to call a method. So `Expr.eval` never reads as a function value, and `e.Num(1)` never reads as a method. The error names the form that works.

A call such as `Expr.eval(e)` has a fix when its first argument has the type. The fix moves that argument before the `.`: `e.eval()`. It is safe when the method takes the other arguments. It is unsafe when one of them does not fit, or stands at a `mut` parameter and has no `mut`. It is unsafe too when a `mut` argument is not a place that can change. It is unsafe too when the method's result does not fit where the call stands. It is also unsafe when the method has type parameters of its own, as in `Tree.gm(t, 1)` for `fn gm<U>(self, u: U)`.

Any other form has no fix, since the value to call the method on is yours to choose. That is, the first argument has another type, is optional or has `mut`, or the call has a label, a type argument or no argument.

## Broken

```sisu
enum Expr {
    Num(i64)
}

fn make(e: Expr) -> Expr {
    e.Num(1)
}

fn main() {
    match make(Expr.Num(0)) {
        Num(n) => print(n)
    }
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
enum Expr {
    Num(i64)
}

fn make(e: Expr) -> Expr {
    match e {
        Num(n) => Expr.Num(n + 1)
    }
}

fn main() {
    match make(Expr.Num(0)) {
        Num(n) => print(n)
    }
}
```
